{
  "Version": 2,
  "Name": "McKenney1970-Fig3a",
  "Source": "D. B. McKenney and P. N. Slater, \"Design and Use of Interference Passband Filters with Wide-Angle Lenses for Multispectral Photography,\" Appl. Opt. 9, 2435-2440 (1970). doi:10.1364/AO.9.002435 - Fig. 3 caption, p. 2437 (lambda1 = 0.7623 lambda0, lambda2 = 1.2785 lambda0); structure from Fig. 2 caption, p. 2436",
  "Notes": "Narrowest of three low-pass:low-pass passband filters showing how the passband width is set by the two stack wavelengths\nSpecification: Passband between the reflection bands of stacks at 0.7623 lambda0 and 1.2785 lambda0; plotted against lambda0/lambda from 0.6 to 1.4.\nReported: Fig. 3 curve a (calculated, normal incidence): transmittance plotted vs lambda0/lambda; high transmittance (~0.9-1.0) roughly over lambda0/lambda ~0.85-1.15 with steep edges and low T outside, the narrowest of the three (read off a perspective plot, approximate).\nNotes: Fig. 3 says the three filters are of 'the same basic design' as Fig. 2 and differ only in the stack layer thicknesses, so assumed: A (n=1.0) | [H/2 L H/2]^7 at lambda1 | M (n=2.10) quarterwave at lambda0 | [H/2 L H/2]^7 at lambda2 | H' (n=2.30) quarterwave at lambda0 | G (n=1.52), with nH = 2.30, nL = 1.38. Thicknesses are QWOT at reference_nm = lambda0: stack 1 layers 0.7623 (H/2 0.38115), stack 2 layers 1.2785 (H/2 0.63925), M and H' 1.0. lambda0 is not given numerically; 553.6 nm is chosen to match Fig. 2 - with constant indices the response is a function of lambda0/lambda only, so any lambda0 reproduces the figure. band_nm is the plotted range 0.6-1.4 lambda0/lambda converted roughly at 553.6 nm. Not exactly specified by the paper: whether the matching-layer indices were also changed (the text says the required matching indices do not change appreciably with filter width).\nLayers from Fig. 3 caption, p. 2437 (lambda1 = 0.7623 lambda0, lambda2 = 1.2785 lambda0); structure from Fig. 2 caption, p. 2436; layer 1 faces the incident medium.\nNot checked: the paper reports this design only as plots.",
